      "README.md": "# Dynamic Circular Buffer & Amortized O(1) Vector\n\n## Overview\n\nA cache-contiguous circular ring buffer and dynamic vector using power-of-two bitmask wrap-around, geometric doubling/halving with hysteresis, and potential-function telemetry.\n\n## Guided Implementation Walkthrough (4 Progressive Steps)\n\n### Step 1: Power-of-2 Bitmask Physical Index Mapping\n\n- **Core Mechanical Concept**: Two’s-Complement Modular Arithmetic via (head + i) & (capacity - 1)\n- **Target Module**: `index.js` (`CircularDeque._physIndex`)\n- **Engineering Specification**: Implement `_physIndex(logicalIdx)` to map any logical offset `0 <= i < size` to its physical slot in `this._buf` without integer division. Because `this._capacity` is strictly a power of two ($2^k$), `this._mask = this._capacity - 1` has its lowest $k$ bits set to `1`. Therefore `(this._head + logicalIdx) & this._mask` computes `(head + i) mod capacity` in 1 CPU cycle, even when `this._head - 1` is negative during `pushFront`.\n- **Verification Invariant**: maps logical indices across wrap-around boundaries via bitmask\n\n### Step 2: Order-Preserving Ring Linearization on Resize\n\n- **Core Mechanical Concept**: Unwrapping Split Ring Segments During Geometric Doubling\n- **Target Module**: `index.js` (`CircularDeque._resize`)\n- **Engineering Specification**: Implement `_resize(newCap)` to allocate a new backing array of length `newCap`, copy all `this._size` live elements in logical order `0 .. size - 1` to indices `0 .. size - 1` of the new buffer, and reset `_head = 0`. Never copy raw physical indices `0 .. cap - 1` directly when `_head !== 0`, or the newly opened empty space will be spliced into the middle of the logical sequence.\n- **Verification Invariant**: linearizes wrapped elements into contiguous order on geometric doubling\n\n### Step 3: Amortized O(1) pushBack and pushFront\n\n- **Core Mechanical Concept**: Circular Head/Tail Advancement & Potential Function Φ = 2n - m\n- **Target Module**: `index.js` (`CircularDeque.pushBack / CircularDeque.pushFront`)\n- **Engineering Specification**: Implement `pushBack(value)` and `pushFront(value)`. 1. If `this._size === this._capacity`, call `this._resize(this._capacity * 2)`. 2. For `pushBack`: write `value` at `(this._head + this._size) & this._mask` and increment `_size`. 3. For `pushFront`: update `this._head = (this._head - 1) & this._mask`, write `value` at `this._head`, and increment `_size`.\n- **Verification Invariant**: supports alternating pushFront and pushBack in amortized O(1) time\n\n### Step 4: popFront, popBack & Quarter-Load Hysteresis Shrinking\n\n- **Core Mechanical Concept**: Preventing Thrashing at Capacity Boundaries via 1/4 Shrink Threshold\n- **Target Module**: `index.js` (`CircularDeque.popFront / CircularDeque.popBack`)\n- **Engineering Specification**: Implement `popFront()` and `popBack()` with GC-friendly slot clearing (`undefined`) and quarter-capacity shrinking. Halving capacity at 50% fullness causes $O(N)$ thrashing on alternating `push`/`pop` sequences at the boundary. Only halve `this._capacity` when `this._capacity > 8 && this._size <= (this._capacity >> 2)`.\n- **Verification Invariant**: pops from both ends and shrinks only at quarter load without thrashing\n\n## Asymptotic & Memory Layout Guarantees\n\n- **Category**: Linear Structures & Memory\n- **Difficulty**: Intermediate (~30 minutes)\n- **Key Architectural Highlights**:\n  - Power-of-2 capacity invariant enabling single-cycle (head + i) & mask wrap-around\n  - Order-preserving geometric doubling (2x) and quarter-load shrinking (0.25x)\n  - Potential-function Φ = 2n - m instrumentation verifying O(1) amortized bounds\n- **Tags & Primitives**: `Circular Buffer`, `Amortized O(1)`, `Bitmask Indexing`, `Dynamic Array`, `Cache Locality`\n\n## Production Systems Context & Verification\n\nThis from-scratch implementation is engineered with zero external dependencies and deterministic verification harnesses. Every internal invariant—from pointer restoration and bitmask addressing to amortized potential bounds and tree height balance—is asserted directly in `test.js`. Use the interactive browser workbench above to complete the guided steps in **Guided Lab** mode or inspect and benchmark the complete reference architecture in **Full Solution** mode.\n\n"
